(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-02"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Physics is a quantitative science: it measures quantities such as length, mass and time. A measured quantity is written as a number followed by a unit, for example 5 m. Because different quantities have different units, a standard system of units, the International System (SI), is used so that everyone can compare results. This chapter covers units, measurement, dimensions, errors and significant figures."},"1.2":{"s":"Earlier systems of units were CGS, MKS and FPS. Since 1971 the SI system has been used all over the world. It has seven fundamental quantities with their units: length (metre), mass (kilogram), time (second), temperature (kelvin), electric current (ampere), luminous intensity (candela) and amount of substance (mole). All other quantities, such as velocity, are derived quantities and their units are derived from these seven."},"1.3":{"s":"Length is measured with a metre scale for everyday objects, and by other methods for very large or very small distances. For large distances like the distance to a planet or star we use the parallax method: the same object is seen from two points a known distance apart and the small angle is measured. The size of a planet is found from the angle it subtends and its distance. Very small sizes are measured with an electron microscope.","f":[{"x":"D = b/theta","n":"Parallax method: D = distance of the object, b = distance between the two observing points, theta = parallax angle in radian (small).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.3, p.4"},{"x":"alpha = d/D","n":"Angular size of a planet: d = diameter of the planet, D = its distance, alpha in radian.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.3, p.4"}],"w":["Put the angle in RADIANS before using D = b/θ; 1 arcsec = 4.85 × 10⁻⁶ rad."]},"1.4":{"s":"The SI unit of mass is the kilogram. Until 2019 it was the mass of a platinum-iridium cylinder kept in France; it is now defined through a fixed value of Planck's constant. For atoms and molecules, where the kilogram is inconvenient, the atomic mass unit is used: one unified atomic mass unit is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom."},"1.5":{"s":"The SI unit of time is the second. It was once defined from the mean solar day, but the length of a day changes slowly, so it is now defined by an atomic clock based on the radiation of the caesium atom. Time is also measured by clocks based on other periodic events."},"1.6":{"s":"The dimensions of a physical quantity show how it depends on the fundamental quantities: mass M, length L, time T, current I, temperature K. Velocity has dimensions [L¹T⁻¹]. Dimensional analysis can check whether an equation is correct, convert units and suggest relations. It cannot give dimensionless constants or handle trigonometric, exponential or logarithmic functions, and it cannot tell if a quantity is a sum of terms.","f":[{"x":"[velocity] = [L T^-1], [acceleration] = [L T^-2], [force] = [M L T^-2]","n":"Dimensions of some common quantities. Energy and work are [M L^2 T^-2], and pressure is [M L^-1 T^-2].","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.6, p.6"}],"w":["An equation can be dimensionally right and still wrong (a missing number such as 1/2 is not caught).","Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted."]},"1.7":{"s":"Accuracy tells how close a measurement is to the true value. Precision tells how close repeated measurements are to each other. A measurement can be precise without being accurate. Uncertainty in an observation comes from the quality of the instrument, the skill of the person, the method used, and the external conditions of the experiment."},"1.8":{"s":"Errors in measurement are systematic or random. Systematic errors push the result in one direction: instrumental, from the method, or personal. Random errors vary unpredictably and are reduced by averaging many readings. The mean of the readings is the most probable value. The absolute error is the difference from the mean, and the mean absolute error is their average. Relative error is that divided by the mean, and percentage error is relative error × 100.","f":[{"x":"a_mean = (a_1 + a_2 + ... + a_n)/n","n":"Arithmetic mean of n readings: the most probable value.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta a_n = |a_mean - a_n|","n":"Absolute error in the n-th reading.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta a_mean = (Delta a_1 + ... + Delta a_n)/n","n":"Mean absolute error.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Relative error = Delta a_mean/a_mean","n":"Divide by the mean value; the percentage error is this times 100.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"Delta Z = Delta A + Delta B","n":"Maximum absolute error in a sum or a difference Z = A + B or Z = A - B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.10"},{"x":"Delta Z/Z = Delta A/A + Delta B/B","n":"Maximum relative error in a product or a quotient.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.10"},{"x":"Delta Z/Z = n*(Delta A/A)","n":"Maximum relative error in Z = A^n. For Z = A^p B^q/C^r: p*(Delta A/A) + q*(Delta B/B) + r*(Delta C/C).","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 1.8, p.11"}],"w":["For both sums AND differences the absolute errors ADD.","For a product, a quotient or a power the RELATIVE errors add, each multiplied by its power."]},"1.9":{"s":"Significant figures are the digits in a measurement that are known reliably, plus one doubtful digit. Their number tells how accurate the measurement is. A reading of 15.43 cm from a scale with a least count of 0.1 cm has the last digit uncertain. Using an instrument with a smaller least count gives more significant figures. In a result, you should not report more figures than the data justify.","w":["The least count of the instrument decides how many figures are significant: a ruler marked in mm gives a result to 0.1 cm."]}};
